27
00:02:03.478 --> 00:02:04.331
Part 1.

28
00:02:04.611 --> 00:02:06.531
Introduction and Why It Matters.

29
00:02:07.781 --> 00:02:10.192
A story: the Diwali double charges.

30
00:02:10.472 --> 00:02:19.304
On the first evening of the Diwali sale, customer support received 1,900 complaints in two hours: charged twice for one order.

31
00:02:19.584 --> 00:02:24.171
The post-incident review found four problems that looked unrelated, and weren't.

32
00:02:25.521 --> 00:02:37.190
The app retried payments. payment-service p99 rose to 4 s under load, and the mobile app's HTTP client timed out at 3 s and retried the POST or payments.

33
00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:39.987
The first request had already charged the card.

34
00:02:40.267 --> 00:02:48.438
The payments API had an Idempotency-Key header, but the implementation looked the key up in Redis before charging and stored it after.

35
00:02:48.718 --> 00:02:56.526
Two requests arriving together (a retry racing the original, or a double-tap on Pay) both found nothing and both charged.

36
00:02:56.806 --> 00:02:58.278
L1 reproduces it:

37
00:02:58.558 --> 00:03:11.977
77 double charges per 1,000 orders with check-then-act, 425 with no idempotency at all, and 0 when the key is claimed with a unique constraint in the same transaction as the charge.

38
00:03:13.327 --> 00:03:15.375
Loyalty points were awarded twice.

39
00:03:15.655 --> 00:03:23.633
The consumer of order.order.placed.v1 restarted during a deployment and re-read the last uncommitted batch of offsets.

40
00:03:23.913 --> 00:03:28.905
A fix from the previous quarter had added a Redis SETNX processed:eventId check.

41
00:03:29.185 --> 00:03:36.780
That fixed the duplicates, but it also meant a crash between the Redis write and the DB write lost points, and nobody noticed.

42
00:03:37.060 --> 00:03:45.785
L2: naive +38,642 points, Redis marker minus 159, inbox table exact.

43
00:03:47.135 --> 00:03:50.058
The settlement job ran twice for store 17.

44
00:03:50.338 --> 00:03:52.962
It used a Redis lock with a 30 s TTL.

45
00:03:53.242 --> 00:03:58.447
A 40 s GC pause (a heap dump on OOM) let a second pod take the lock.

46
00:03:58.727 --> 00:04:02.482
The first pod woke up and finished its settlement, and the payout was doubled.

47
00:04:02.762 --> 00:04:10.079
L4 shows why a TTL lock alone can't prevent this, and how fencing tokens or a database-held lock do.

48
00:04:10.359 --> 00:04:12.663
The orders table got slower every week.

49
00:04:12.943 --> 00:04:20.517
It used random UUIDv4 primary keys, and once the index outgrew memory, every insert touched a random index page.

50
00:04:20.797 --> 00:04:21.650
L3:

51
00:04:21.930 --> 00:04:32.063
25.3 s versus 10.8 s for 4 million inserts, and 1.5 GB versus 0.7 GB of WAL.

52
00:04:33.413 --> 00:04:34.395
The fixes:

53
00:04:34.675 --> 00:04:38.451
An idempotency record claimed atomically with the business effect.

54
00:04:38.731 --> 00:04:42.187
An inbox table in the consumer's database transaction.

55
00:04:42.467 --> 00:04:48.931
Fencing tokens checked by the database, or better, no lock at all where a conditional update suffices.

56
00:04:49.211 --> 00:04:53.115
Time-ordered IDs (UUIDv7 or Snowflake) for new tables.

57
00:04:54.465 --> 00:04:55.147
Quote:

58
00:04:55.427 --> 00:04:57.091
The thesis of this chapter.

59
00:04:57.371 --> 00:05:06.417
In a distributed system every message and every request can arrive zero, one or many times, and you can't tell a slow reply from a lost one.

60
00:05:06.697 --> 00:05:09.385
Exactly once is a myth at the transport level.

61
00:05:09.665 --> 00:05:19.777
It exists only as at-least-once delivery + an idempotent effect, and the idempotency must be enforced by the system of record, atomically with the effect.

62
00:05:20.057 --> 00:05:28.249
The same principle makes locks safe: the resource must reject stale holders (fencing), because the holder can't know it has been paused.

63
00:05:29.599 --> 00:05:31.903
