118
00:08:13.147 --> 00:08:14.022
Part 2.

119
00:08:14.302 --> 00:08:16.179
Core Concepts, Beginner to Deep.

120
00:08:17.429 --> 00:08:18.347
Intuition.

121
00:08:18.627 --> 00:08:20.675
Idempotency is the lift button.

122
00:08:20.955 --> 00:08:23.344
Pressing it five times calls the lift once.

123
00:08:23.624 --> 00:08:28.061
Add 100 rupeesto the wallet pressed twice adds 200 rupees.

124
00:08:28.341 --> 00:08:32.288
Set this top-up request's status to PAID pressed twice is still PAID.

125
00:08:32.568 --> 00:08:42.467
The trick is to turn the second kind of operation into the third kind, by giving each request a name (an idempotency key) and remembering which names have been processed.

126
00:08:43.817 --> 00:08:45.950
IDs are token numbers at a bakery.

127
00:08:46.230 --> 00:08:52.587
One machine hands out numbers: simple and ordered, but everyone queues at one machine (a DB sequence).

128
00:08:52.867 --> 00:09:02.019
Or every counter prints its own numbers: counter 3's next number is 3 to 0457 (Snowflake: time + worker + sequence).

129
00:09:02.299 --> 00:09:12.369
Or everyone picks a random 10-digit number: no coordination, but no order, and short numbers collide sooner than you think (UUIDv4, the birthday paradox).

130
00:09:13.719 --> 00:09:16.321
A lock with a TTL is a parking ticket.

131
00:09:16.601 --> 00:09:18.116
It's valid for two hours.

132
00:09:18.396 --> 00:09:24.903
If you fall asleep in the car and wake up after three, someone else has legally been given your spot, and you don't know it.

133
00:09:25.183 --> 00:09:32.991
Unless the parking attendant checks the ticket's serial number before you drive off (a fencing token), you both believe the spot is yours.

134
00:09:34.341 --> 00:09:36.239
Idempotency: definitions.

135
00:09:36.519 --> 00:09:45.159
An operation is idempotent if performing it many times has the same effect as performing it once: f(f(x)) = f(x).

136
00:09:46.509 --> 00:09:47.853
Here is a quick table:

137
00:09:48.133 --> 00:09:51.632
Operation, and Idempotent?, and Make it idempotent by....

138
00:09:51.912 --> 00:09:53.640
GET or orders or 42.

139
00:09:53.920 --> 00:09:54.944
Idempotent?:

140
00:09:55.224 --> 00:09:56.653
Yes (safe, no effect).

141
00:09:56.933 --> 00:10:00.197
PUT or orders or 42 or address (full replacement).

142
00:10:00.477 --> 00:10:01.501
Idempotent?:

143
00:10:01.781 --> 00:10:02.507
Yes.

144
00:10:02.787 --> 00:10:05.645
DELETE or carts or 7 or items or 3.

145
00:10:05.925 --> 00:10:06.949
Idempotent?:

146
00:10:07.229 --> 00:10:12.755
Yes (the second call is a no-op; it may return 404, but the state is the same).

147
00:10:13.035 --> 00:10:14.229
POST or payments.

148
00:10:14.509 --> 00:10:15.533
Idempotent?:

149
00:10:15.813 --> 00:10:16.496
No.

150
00:10:16.776 --> 00:10:18.376
Make it idempotent by...:

151
00:10:18.656 --> 00:10:19.936
Idempotency key.

152
00:10:20.216 --> 00:10:23.437
UPDATE stock SET available = available - 2.

153
00:10:23.717 --> 00:10:24.741
Idempotent?:

154
00:10:25.021 --> 00:10:25.704
No.

155
00:10:25.984 --> 00:10:27.584
Make it idempotent by...:

156
00:10:27.864 --> 00:10:31.085
Condition on a version, or an inbox or dedup table.

157
00:10:32.435 --> 00:10:36.211
UPDATE order SET status = 'PACKED' WHERE status = 'PICKED'.

158
00:10:36.491 --> 00:10:37.515
Idempotent?:

159
00:10:37.795 --> 00:10:39.694
Yes (a conditional transition).

160
00:10:39.974 --> 00:10:41.318
INSERT INTO charge...

161
00:10:41.598 --> 00:10:42.622
Idempotent?:

162
00:10:42.902 --> 00:10:43.585
No.

163
00:10:43.865 --> 00:10:45.465
Make it idempotent by...:

164
00:10:45.745 --> 00:10:48.902
Unique constraint on a natural or idempotency key.

165
00:10:50.252 --> 00:10:54.305
HTTP semantics (RFC 9110):

166
00:10:54.585 --> 00:11:00.409
GET, HEAD, PUT, DELETE and OPTIONS are defined as idempotent, and POST and PATCH are not.

167
00:11:00.689 --> 00:11:09.735
Clients, proxies and gateways may automatically retry idempotent methods, so a PUT that isn't really idempotent is a bug waiting for a retry.

168
00:11:10.015 --> 00:11:17.972
Why retries are unavoidable: a timeout doesn't tell you whether the request was processed (Ch 17 §2.4).

169
00:11:18.252 --> 00:11:22.924
The client must retry to get an answer, and the server must tolerate the duplicate.

170
00:11:23.204 --> 00:11:32.143
L1: with 30 % of responses lost, the naive API created 1,657 charges for 1,000 orders.

171
00:11:33.493 --> 00:11:35.178
The idempotency-key pattern.

172
00:11:35.458 --> 00:11:52.589
The client generates a unique key per logical operation (one checkout attempt, not one HTTP attempt) and sends it as a header (Idempotency-Key, standardised in an IETF HTTPAPI draft and used by Stripe and Razorpay-style APIs).

173
00:11:52.869 --> 00:11:54.703
The server keeps a record per key:

174
00:11:56.053 --> 00:11:57.653
Here is the SQL code.

175
00:12:00.503 --> 00:12:01.506
The protocol:

176
00:12:01.786 --> 00:12:04.922
Claim atomically: insert... on conflict do nothing.

177
00:12:05.202 --> 00:12:07.335
Only one request can insert the row.

178
00:12:07.615 --> 00:12:11.391
If the claim succeeded: perform the effect and store the response.

179
00:12:11.671 --> 00:12:17.517
For effects in the same database, do it in the same transaction as the claim (L1 atomic).

180
00:12:17.797 --> 00:12:25.839
Then a crash rolls back both, and a concurrent duplicate blocks on the unique index until the first commits, then sees the stored response.

181
00:12:26.119 --> 00:12:27.719
If the key already exists:

182
00:12:29.069 --> 00:12:35.768
Different fingerprint, then 422 (the key was reused for a different request; L1 shows this).

183
00:12:36.048 --> 00:12:39.013
COMPLETED, then replay the stored status and body.

184
00:12:39.293 --> 00:12:46.845
INPROGRESS (possible when the claim and the effect aren't in one transaction), then 409 Conflict with Retry-After.

185
00:12:47.125 --> 00:12:54.080
Expire records after a retention period longer than any client's retry window (24 h to 7 days).

186
00:12:54.360 --> 00:12:56.664
Clients must not reuse keys after that.

187
00:12:58.014 --> 00:12:59.571
Why check-then-act fails:

188
00:12:59.851 --> 00:13:01.899
SELECT finds nothing, then INSERT.

189
00:13:02.179 --> 00:13:04.505
Two concurrent requests both pass the check.

190
00:13:04.785 --> 00:13:05.617
L1:

191
00:13:05.897 --> 00:13:09.566
77 of 100 double-clicks still double-charged.

192
00:13:09.846 --> 00:13:17.142
Only a unique constraint (or an atomic SET NX, if the whole effect lives in the same store) closes the race.

193
00:13:17.422 --> 00:13:18.531
The client's side:

194
00:13:18.811 --> 00:13:27.366
Generate the key before the first attempt, persist it with the pending operation (so an app restart reuses it), and reuse it on every retry.

195
00:13:28.716 --> 00:13:32.129
If all retries fail, the outcome is unknown, not failed.

196
00:13:32.409 --> 00:13:38.831
L1's atomic run had 12 clients give up, but all 1,000 orders were charged exactly once.

197
00:13:39.111 --> 00:13:44.828
The app must show payment pending and query status later with the same key, never create a new payment.

198
00:13:46.178 --> 00:13:48.610
Idempotency with external side effects.

199
00:13:48.890 --> 00:13:56.719
When the effect is a call to another system (the payment gateway, an SMS provider), it can't share your database transaction.

200
00:13:56.999 --> 00:14:01.437
The pattern is a state machine with recovery points (Stripe's atomic phases):

201
00:14:01.717 --> 00:14:07.327
Transaction 1: claim the key, create payment(status=INITIATED), and commit.

202
00:14:07.607 --> 00:14:15.735
Call the gateway, passing a deterministic idempotency key (for example, paymentid), so the gateway deduplicates your retries.

203
00:14:16.015 --> 00:14:25.061
Transaction 2: record the gateway's result, set status=CAPTURED or FAILED, store the response on the idempotency record, and commit.

204
00:14:26.411 --> 00:14:31.040
A retry finds the record INPROGRESS with payment.status=INITIATED.

205
00:14:31.320 --> 00:14:43.821
It either resumes from step 2 (safe because the gateway deduplicates by key), or returns 409 while a background reconciler resolves stuck INITIATED payments by querying the gateway.

206
00:14:44.101 --> 00:14:49.349
Rule: every hop must be idempotent under the same logical key, or the chain isn't.

207
00:14:49.629 --> 00:15:02.237
When a downstream API has no idempotency support, you need query-before-retry (did payment X happen?) and reconciliation (Ch 13 §2.7, Ch 45).

208
00:15:03.587 --> 00:15:05.913
Idempotent consumers: the inbox.

209
00:15:06.193 --> 00:15:13.339
Kafka, SQS, RabbitMQ and NATS JetStream all deliver at least once to consumers (Ch 14).

210
00:15:13.619 --> 00:15:14.985
Duplicates come from:

211
00:15:15.265 --> 00:15:16.502
Producer retries.

212
00:15:16.782 --> 00:15:19.342
L2's log had 97 duplicates.

213
00:15:19.622 --> 00:15:27.665
Kafka's idempotent producer removes duplicates caused by broker-level retries, but not an application that publishes twice.

214
00:15:27.945 --> 00:15:33.705
Redelivery after a crash or rebalance, when processing succeeded but the offset commit didn't.

215
00:15:33.985 --> 00:15:38.635
L2 had 9 to 15 crashes with commits every 100 messages.

216
00:15:38.915 --> 00:15:41.710
Replays and reprocessing (resetting offsets).

217
00:15:43.060 --> 00:15:45.577
The consumer must make its effect idempotent.

218
00:15:45.857 --> 00:15:47.585
The options, from strongest:

219
00:15:48.935 --> 00:15:50.279
Here is a quick table:

220
00:15:50.559 --> 00:15:52.735
Approach, and How, and L2 result.

221
00:15:53.015 --> 00:15:55.213
Inbox table in the same transaction.

222
00:15:55.493 --> 00:16:03.322
How: insert into inbox(consumer, eventid) on conflict do nothing; apply the effect only if inserted; commit both.

223
00:16:03.602 --> 00:16:04.711
L2 result:

224
00:16:04.991 --> 00:16:05.845
Exact.

225
00:16:06.125 --> 00:16:07.895
Naturally idempotent effect.

226
00:16:08.175 --> 00:16:15.557
How: set status = 'PAID' where orderid =? and status 'PAID'; upserts keyed by event id; version checks.

227
00:16:15.837 --> 00:16:16.946
