330
00:21:00.725 --> 00:21:03.583
L3's surprise, with 8 concurrent writers:

331
00:21:03.863 --> 00:21:12.823
Time-ordered keys (Snowflake, UUIDv7) had only ~61 % leaf density, below random UUIDv4's 71 %.

332
00:21:13.103 --> 00:21:21.061
Concurrent batches interleave just behind the right edge of the index, and PostgreSQL splits those pages 50 or 50.

333
00:21:21.341 --> 00:21:25.479
The inserts are still fast, because the working set is the hot right edge.

334
00:21:25.759 --> 00:21:30.943
With a single writer (negative control), all ordered schemes had 90 % density.

335
00:21:31.223 --> 00:21:38.178
A DB-assigned identity (assigned at insert time, so strictly increasing) had 91 % either way.

336
00:21:39.528 --> 00:21:40.979
Snowflake in practice.

337
00:21:41.259 --> 00:21:43.605
Worker-id assignment is the hard part.

338
00:21:43.885 --> 00:21:44.845
The options:

339
00:21:45.125 --> 00:21:47.173
The Kubernetes StatefulSet ordinal.

340
00:21:47.453 --> 00:21:53.341
A lease in etcd or ZooKeeper, where the worker id is held as long as the lease (Ch 12).

341
00:21:53.621 --> 00:21:59.125
A DB table with workerid rows claimed via for update skip locked plus a heartbeat.

342
00:21:59.405 --> 00:22:01.667
A random id with collision detection.

343
00:22:03.017 --> 00:22:06.238
Never use hash of the hostname: two pods can collide.

344
00:22:06.518 --> 00:22:13.579
L3: two pods with the same worker id collided on 20 to 39 % of ids (it varies by run).

345
00:22:13.859 --> 00:22:14.563
Clock:

346
00:22:14.843 --> 00:22:25.083
Use the wall clock (IDs must be meaningful across restarts), guard against it going backwards (NTP steps), and never go below the last issued timestamp.

347
00:22:25.363 --> 00:22:29.267
Small rollbacks: wait until the last ms (L3 guarded:

348
00:22:29.547 --> 00:22:30.849
0 duplicates).

349
00:22:31.129 --> 00:22:33.561
Large rollbacks: refuse and alert.

350
00:22:34.911 --> 00:22:40.095
Persist the last timestamp across restarts if restarts can be faster than a rollback.

351
00:22:40.375 --> 00:22:46.327
Sequence exhaustion: past 4,096 ids in a ms, wait for the next ms.

352
00:22:46.607 --> 00:22:49.999
At a million ids per second per worker, add more workers.

353
00:22:50.279 --> 00:22:57.297
Epoch and bits: a custom epoch (2025 to 01 to 01) gives 69 years.

354
00:22:57.577 --> 00:23:01.908
Adjust the split (more worker bits versus sequence bits) to the topology.

355
00:23:02.188 --> 00:23:05.089
Sortability is per worker and to the millisecond.

356
00:23:05.369 --> 00:23:09.700
Across workers, ordering is only as good as clock sync (Ch 12).

357
00:23:09.980 --> 00:23:13.137
Don't use ID order as event order for correctness.

358
00:23:14.487 --> 00:23:16.109
Order numbers customers see.

359
00:23:16.389 --> 00:23:21.893
Keep the internal primary key (UUIDv7 or bigint) separate from the public reference:

360
00:23:22.173 --> 00:23:24.989
The format: readable, and meaningful to support.

361
00:23:25.269 --> 00:23:40.650
For example, BLR17 to 250925 to 7KQ4M: store code, date, 5 base32 chars (25 bits) random, with a unique constraint and a retry on conflict.

362
00:23:40.930 --> 00:23:50.274
Per store per day, 25 bits (33M values) hits its first collision around ~7K orders, which the retry handles cheaply.

363
00:23:50.554 --> 00:23:54.309
Not enumerable: don't expose sequential IDs in URLs.

364
00:23:54.589 --> 00:24:00.989
Authorization checks are still required (Ch 23), because obscurity is only defence in depth.

365
00:24:02.339 --> 00:24:05.773
Check digit (Luhn or Damm) when codes are typed by hand.

366
00:24:07.123 --> 00:24:09.385
Distributed locks: what they are for.

367
00:24:09.665 --> 00:24:12.758
Following Martin Kleppmann, a lock is used for either:

368
00:24:13.038 --> 00:24:18.414
Efficiency: avoid doing the same work twice (two pods sending the same daily report).

369
00:24:18.694 --> 00:24:23.174
An occasional duplicate is harmless, so a simple TTL lock is fine.

370
00:24:24.524 --> 00:24:28.983
Correctness: two holders at once corrupt data (a double settlement payout).

371
00:24:29.263 --> 00:24:38.692
Then the lock alone can't be trusted, because a holder can be paused (GC, VM steal, page faults, network delay) past its lease and not know it.

372
00:24:38.972 --> 00:24:47.847
You need fencing: every lease gets a monotonically increasing token, and the resource rejects operations from tokens older than one it has seen.

