423
00:26:43.278 --> 00:26:45.006
Fencing tokens, done right.

424
00:26:45.286 --> 00:26:46.822
L4 found a subtlety.

425
00:26:47.102 --> 00:26:54.974
Check the token on write (update... set value=? fence=? where fence:token) isn't enough for read-modify-write:

426
00:26:55.254 --> 00:26:58.241
Holder A (token 5) reads 0 and pauses.

427
00:26:58.521 --> 00:27:01.593
The lease expires, and B (token 6) reads 0.

428
00:27:01.873 --> 00:27:03.323
A wakes and writes 1.

429
00:27:03.603 --> 00:27:07.529
Its fence 5 is greater than the stored 4, so the write is accepted.

430
00:27:07.809 --> 00:27:10.475
B writes 1 with fence 6, also accepted.

431
00:27:11.825 --> 00:27:13.084
One increment lost.

432
00:27:13.364 --> 00:27:15.711
The random runs rarely hit this window.

433
00:27:15.991 --> 00:27:18.636
The scripted run in L4 hits it every time.

434
00:27:18.916 --> 00:27:30.415
The fix: claim the resource with the token before reading (update... set fence =:token where fence:token returning value), and make the write conditional on fence =:token.

435
00:27:30.695 --> 00:27:34.193
A's write is then rejected, and it retries with a new lease.

436
00:27:34.473 --> 00:27:39.572
More generally, the resource must see the token on every access within the critical section.

437
00:27:40.922 --> 00:27:47.066
Even simpler: if the resource supports a conditional write (CAS), you often don't need a lock at all.

438
00:27:47.346 --> 00:27:59.058
L4's update... where value =:read with retry lost 0 updates, and it was the fastest safe option (1.8 s versus 5 to 7 s for the locked variants).

439
00:28:00.408 --> 00:28:01.944
The best lock is no lock.

440
00:28:02.224 --> 00:28:06.640
Before reaching for a distributed lock, look for a design that doesn't need one:

441
00:28:06.920 --> 00:28:14.301
Atomic statements: update stock set available = available -:n where sku =? and available =:n.

442
00:28:14.581 --> 00:28:18.933
Unique constraints: at most one active reservation per (orderid, sku).

443
00:28:19.213 --> 00:28:27.683
Optimistic concurrency: a version column (JPA @Version), ETags or If-Match in APIs (Ch 04).

444
00:28:27.963 --> 00:28:39.056
Single writer per key: partition by key so one consumer owns each store's settlement stream (Kafka partitioning, Ch 11 or 14), or an actor per aggregate.

445
00:28:40.406 --> 00:28:50.774
Work queues: select... for update skip locked hands each job to exactly one worker, inside the database that holds the job (Ch 13 outbox relay).

446
00:28:51.054 --> 00:28:59.203
Leader election with fencing for singleton jobs: a consensus-backed lease plus a token checked by the resource (Ch 12).

