00:31:09.861 --> 00:31:11.184
Related ADRs:

488
00:31:11.464 --> 00:31:37.832
ADR-083 Idempotency keys on all non-idempotent public APIs; ADR-084 Inbox table for every consumer with side effects; ADR-085 UUIDv7 default PK, Snowflake for event ids; ADR-086 No correctness locks without fencing; prefer CAS or constraints; ADR-087 Public order references separate from PKs.

489
00:31:39.182 --> 00:31:39.822
1.

490
00:31:40.102 --> 00:31:41.510
Context and Problem.

491
00:31:41.790 --> 00:31:56.958
The Diwali incident (§1.1): double charges from check-then-act idempotency, double and lost loyalty points, a double settlement from a TTL lock and a GC pause, and degrading insert performance from UUIDv4 keys.

492
00:31:57.238 --> 00:31:57.942
2.

493
00:31:58.222 --> 00:31:59.566
Goals or Non-Goals.

494
00:31:59.846 --> 00:32:14.417
Goals: no duplicate effects from retries, redelivery or double submission; no lost effects; IDs that are unique without coordination, index-friendly and safe to expose; singleton jobs that are safe under pauses.

495
00:32:15.767 --> 00:32:21.335
Non-goals: exactly-once delivery at the transport level; global total order of IDs.

496
00:32:21.615 --> 00:32:22.361
3.

497
00:32:22.641 --> 00:32:23.601
Requirements.

498
00:32:24.951 --> 00:32:26.295
Here is a quick table:

499
00:32:26.575 --> 00:32:28.047
ID, and Requirement.

500
00:32:28.327 --> 00:32:29.266
FR-1:

501
00:32:29.546 --> 00:32:39.274
Every public POST or PATCH that creates or moves money, stock or orders accepts Idempotency-Key; replays return the original status and body.

502
00:32:39.554 --> 00:32:40.493
FR-2:

503
00:32:40.773 --> 00:32:50.778
Key reuse with a different request, then 422; concurrent duplicate, then wait-and-replay (same DB) or 409 + Retry-After.

504
00:32:51.058 --> 00:32:52.039
FR-3:

505
00:32:52.319 --> 00:32:58.997
Every Kafka consumer with side effects records (consumer, eventid) in the same transaction as the effect.

506
00:32:59.277 --> 00:33:00.322
NFR-1:

507
00:33:00.602 --> 00:33:07.087
0 duplicate charges in the chaos test (30 % lost responses, 10 % double submits).

508
00:33:07.367 --> 00:33:08.413
NFR-2:

509
00:33:08.693 --> 00:33:13.002
Idempotency overhead at most 2 ms p99 per request.

510
00:33:13.282 --> 00:33:14.349
NFR-3:

511
00:33:14.629 --> 00:33:21.583
Idempotency records retained 7 days; inbox rows retained ≥ topic retention.

512
00:33:22.933 --> 00:33:24.021
NFR-4:

513
00:33:24.301 --> 00:33:33.048
Correctness locks must use fencing tokens verified by the resource; CI rule flags SET NX used for money-moving jobs.

514
00:33:34.398 --> 00:33:35.102
4.

515
00:33:35.382 --> 00:33:36.662
Capacity estimates.

516
00:33:38.012 --> 00:33:39.356
Here is a quick table:

517
00:33:39.636 --> 00:33:41.940
Item, and Calculation, and Result.

518
00:33:42.220 --> 00:33:43.628
Idempotency records.

519
00:33:43.908 --> 00:33:44.975
Calculation:

520
00:33:45.255 --> 00:33:52.188
150 payments per second peak times 86,400 times 7 days (upper bound).

521
00:33:52.468 --> 00:33:57.631
Result: at most 90M rows or week; partition by day, drop old partitions.

522
00:33:57.911 --> 00:33:58.956
Record size.

523
00:33:59.236 --> 00:34:06.361
Calculation: key 36 B + fingerprint 16 B + response ~300 B + overhead.

524
00:34:06.641 --> 00:34:14.087
Result: ~0.5 KB, then at most 45 GB or week at the peak rate, far less on average.

525
00:34:14.367 --> 00:34:15.668
Inbox (loyalty).

526
00:34:15.948 --> 00:34:17.015
Calculation:

527
00:34:17.295 --> 00:34:19.556
30 events per second times 30 days.

528
00:34:19.836 --> 00:34:20.647
Result:

529
00:34:20.927 --> 00:34:24.809
78M rows, ~6 GB; daily partitions.

530
00:34:25.089 --> 00:34:26.540
Snowflake capacity.

531
00:34:26.820 --> 00:34:27.887
Calculation:

532
00:34:28.167 --> 00:34:30.812
4,096 per ms per worker.

533
00:34:31.092 --> 00:34:31.903
Result:

534
00:34:32.183 --> 00:34:34.956
4M ids per second per worker, ample.

535
00:34:35.236 --> 00:34:36.601
Order code collisions.

536
00:34:36.881 --> 00:34:37.948
Calculation:

537
00:34:38.228 --> 00:34:42.857
25 random bits per store per day, 3,000 orders or store per day.

538
00:34:43.137 --> 00:34:43.948
Result:

539
00:34:44.228 --> 00:34:51.759
P(any collision) about n² or 2N about 13 % per store-day, handled by a unique-constraint retry.

540
00:34:53.109 --> 00:34:53.877
5.

541
00:34:54.157 --> 00:34:55.351
Proposed design.

542
00:34:55.631 --> 00:35:16.837
An idempotency Spring module: a filter + annotation (@Idempotent(scope = customer)) that claims the key in the service's own database (same transaction as the effect, via TransactionTemplate), fingerprints the canonical request, replays stored responses and returns 422 or 409.

543
00:35:17.117 --> 00:35:18.311
Daily partitions.

544
00:35:18.591 --> 00:35:29.407
External calls: payment state machine + a deterministic downstream key (paymentid) + a reconciler job (every 1 min for INITIATED older than 2 min).

545
00:35:30.757 --> 00:35:37.968
An inbox module for consumers: @IdempotentConsumer wraps the listener in a transaction with the inbox insert.

546
00:35:38.248 --> 00:35:40.659
Offsets are committed after the transaction.

547
00:35:40.939 --> 00:35:56.917
IDs: the ids library provides UUIDv7 (application-side) and Snowflake, with worker ids leased from etcd (TTL 30 s, renewed) and clock-rollback guards. bigint identity stays allowed for tables private to one database.

548
00:35:58.267 --> 00:36:08.059
Coordination: leader election in etcd with fencing tokens stored and checked in PostgreSQL (Ch 12) for settlement and reconciliation.

549
00:36:08.339 --> 00:36:12.670
PostgreSQL advisory locks for jobs local to one database.

550
00:36:12.950 --> 00:36:15.147
ShedLock only for efficiency jobs.

551
00:36:15.427 --> 00:36:16.174
7.

552
00:36:16.454 --> 00:36:17.969
Alternatives considered.

553
00:36:19.319 --> 00:36:20.663
Here is a quick table:

554
00:36:20.943 --> 00:36:23.481
Option, and Pros, and Cons, and Verdict.

555
00:36:23.761 --> 00:36:26.428
Idempotency in Redis (SET NX).

556
00:36:26.708 --> 00:36:27.561
Pros:

557
00:36:27.841 --> 00:36:28.887
Fast, shared.

558
00:36:29.167 --> 00:36:29.977
Cons:

559
00:36:30.257 --> 00:36:35.441
Not atomic with the DB effect: duplicates or lost effects (L1 or L2).

560
00:36:35.721 --> 00:36:36.532
Verdict:

561
00:36:36.812 --> 00:36:38.647
Rejected for money or stock.

562
00:36:38.927 --> 00:36:42.596
Idempotency record in the service DB, same transaction.

563
00:36:42.876 --> 00:36:43.729
Pros:

564
00:36:44.009 --> 00:36:45.119
Atomic, simple.

565
00:36:45.399 --> 00:36:46.209
Cons:

566
00:36:46.489 --> 00:36:48.687
DB load, retention management.

567
00:36:48.967 --> 00:36:49.777
Verdict:

568
00:36:50.057 --> 00:36:50.889
Chosen.

569
00:36:51.169 --> 00:36:52.961
Gateway-level idempotency.

570
00:36:53.241 --> 00:36:54.095
Pros:

571
00:36:54.375 --> 00:36:55.271
One place.

572
00:36:55.551 --> 00:36:56.361
Cons:

573
00:36:56.641 --> 00:36:59.927
The gateway can't know if the effect happened; not atomic.

574
00:37:00.207 --> 00:37:01.017
Verdict:

575
00:37:01.297 --> 00:37:03.729
Rejected (a cache of responses only).

576
00:37:04.009 --> 00:37:05.268
UUIDv4 PKs.

577
00:37:05.548 --> 00:37:06.401
Pros:

578
00:37:06.681 --> 00:37:08.047
Simple, unguessable.

579
00:37:08.327 --> 00:37:09.137
Cons:

580
00:37:09.417 --> 00:37:12.233
Index or WAL cost at scale (L3).

581
00:37:12.513 --> 00:37:13.324
Verdict:

582
00:37:13.604 --> 00:37:14.735
Legacy only.

583
00:37:15.015 --> 00:37:16.039
UUIDv7.

584
00:37:16.319 --> 00:37:17.172
Pros:

585
00:37:17.452 --> 00:37:19.159
Ordered, no coordination.

586
00:37:19.439 --> 00:37:20.249
Cons:

587
00:37:20.529 --> 00:37:23.495
16 bytes; timestamp leaks creation time.

588
00:37:23.775 --> 00:37:24.585
Verdict:

589
00:37:24.865 --> 00:37:25.719
Default.

590
00:37:27.069 --> 00:37:28.413
Redlock for settlement.

591
00:37:28.693 --> 00:37:29.546
Pros:

592
00:37:29.826 --> 00:37:31.383
Uses existing Redis.

593
00:37:31.663 --> 00:37:32.474
Cons:

594
00:37:32.754 --> 00:37:34.866
No fencing, timing assumptions.

595
00:37:35.146 --> 00:37:35.957
Verdict:

596
00:37:36.237 --> 00:37:37.154
Rejected.

597
00:37:38.504 --> 00:37:39.187
8.

598
00:37:39.467 --> 00:37:40.619
Failure modes.

599
00:37:40.899 --> 00:37:46.083
Idempotency DB unavailable, then the API fails closed (503).

600
00:37:46.363 --> 00:37:48.325
We never process without the record.

601
00:37:48.605 --> 00:37:54.429
Fingerprint canonicalisation bugs, then false 422s, so it has contract tests.

602
00:37:54.709 --> 00:37:58.976
Worker-id lease lost, then the generator stops issuing until it's re-leased.

603
00:37:59.256 --> 00:38:03.053
Clock step back 1 s, then the generator refuses and alerts.

604
00:38:03.333 --> 00:38:04.037
10.

605
00:38:04.317 --> 00:38:05.405
Observability.

606
00:38:06.755 --> 00:38:14.350
Idempotency: replays per second by endpoint, 422s, 409s, and records stuck INPROGRESS.

607
00:38:14.630 --> 00:38:16.913
Inbox: duplicate rate per consumer.

608
00:38:17.193 --> 00:38:20.627
Fencing: rejections (each one is an averted incident).

609
00:38:20.907 --> 00:38:22.806
Snowflake: clock-rollback waits.

610
00:38:23.086 --> 00:38:25.987
Unknown-outcome payments pending reconciliation.

611
00:38:27.337 --> 00:38:28.191
14.

612
00:38:28.471 --> 00:38:32.695
Decision (ADR-083 to 087 summary).

613
00:38:32.975 --> 00:38:35.620
Idempotency records atomically with the effect.

614
00:38:35.900 --> 00:38:37.351
Inbox for consumers.

615
00:38:37.631 --> 00:38:41.193
Downstream keys and reconciliation for external effects.

616
00:38:41.473 --> 00:38:42.967
UUIDv7 by default.

617
00:38:43.247 --> 00:38:45.487
Fencing or no lock for correctness.

618
00:38:45.767 --> 00:38:48.113
Public references separate from PKs.

619
00:38:49.463 --> 00:38:50.423
Part 5.

620
00:38:50.703 --> 00:38:52.687
Hands-On Code, Level Ladder.

621
00:38:53.937 --> 00:38:55.281
Here is a quick table:

622
00:38:55.561 --> 00:38:58.249
Level, and New requirement that forces the step.

623
00:38:58.529 --> 00:38:59.361
L1:

624
00:38:59.641 --> 00:39:02.521
Retries and double-taps must never double-charge.

625
00:39:02.801 --> 00:39:06.876
(idempotency keys, real HTTP + PostgreSQL).

626
00:39:07.156 --> 00:39:07.967
L2:

627
00:39:08.247 --> 00:39:12.065
Consumers crash and messages repeat; points must be exact.

628
00:39:12.345 --> 00:39:15.225
(inbox, real PostgreSQL + Redis).

629
00:39:15.505 --> 00:39:16.359
L3:

630
00:39:16.639 --> 00:39:20.991
Pick primary keys for 100M-row tables and IDs for events.

631
00:39:21.271 --> 00:39:25.409
(real PostgreSQL insert benchmark + generator properties).

632
00:39:25.689 --> 00:39:26.564
L4:

633
00:39:26.844 --> 00:39:30.684
Only one settlement may run per store, even with GC pauses.

634
00:39:30.964 --> 00:39:33.887
(Redis locks, fencing, advisory locks, CAS).

635
00:39:35.237 --> 00:39:51.493
All four levels run against real PostgreSQL 16 (:5542, database shop, started by setup.sh) and real Redis 7 (:6390), with Java 21 and the pgjdbc driver.

636
00:39:51.773 --> 00:39:52.583
Machine:

637
00:39:52.863 --> 00:39:54.549
2 vCPU container.

638
00:39:54.829 --> 00:39:56.706
The outputs below are verbatim.

639
00:39:56.986 --> 00:40:01.487
Lab files are in labs or ch20-idempotency-ids-locks/.

640
00:40:02.837 --> 00:40:05.376
L1, An idempotent payment API.

641
00:40:05.656 --> 00:40:06.595
Requirement:

642
00:40:06.875 --> 00:40:11.312
Retries after timeouts and double-taps on Pay must never double-charge.

643
00:40:11.592 --> 00:40:22.195
A real HTTP server (JDK HttpServer, virtual threads) exposes POST /strategy/charges backed by PostgreSQL.

644
00:40:22.475 --> 00:40:31.605
To simulate lost responses, 30 % of responses are delayed past the client's 250 ms timeout after the transaction committed.

645
00:40:31.885 --> 00:40:35.832
The client retries up to 4 times with the same Idempotency-Key.

646
00:40:36.112 --> 00:40:40.763
10 % of orders are double-clicked: two concurrent requests with the same key.

647
00:40:41.043 --> 00:40:45.736
1,000 orders per strategy. naive: insert a charge per request.

648
00:40:47.086 --> 00:41:00.377
check-then-act: select the key, then charge, then insert the key (no unique constraint, separate statements). atomic: insert... on conflict do nothing on the key's primary key, and the charge, in one transaction.

649
00:41:00.657 --> 00:41:06.395
A duplicate replays the stored status and body, and a different fingerprint gets 422.

650
00:41:07.745 --> 00:41:11.052
Here is the Java code, IdempotencyL1.java.

651
00:41:11.332 --> 00:41:15.599
It runs to 243 lines; the full listing is in the chapter.

652
00:41:15.879 --> 00:41:31.431
IdempotencyL1.java, run with: java -Duser.timezone=UTC -Dsun.net.httpserver.nodelay=true -cp or tmp or pgjdbc.jar IdempotencyL1.java.

653
00:41:31.711 --> 00:41:43.359
A real HTTP charge API (JDK HttpServer, virtual threads) over real PostgreSQL (:5542, db shop).

654
00:41:43.639 --> 00:41:51.681
The client retries when a response is lost (30 % of responses arrive after the client's 250 ms timeout).

655
00:41:54.531 --> 00:41:55.449
On screen:

656
00:41:55.729 --> 00:42:28.405
1,000 orders paid over real HTTP + PostgreSQL; 30 % of responses lost (client retries. same key); 10 % double-clicked. strategy charges extra orders charged client saw gave up. created charges more than once success. naive 1,657 657 425 1,089 11. check-then-act 1,077 77 77 1,091 9. atomic

657
00:42:28.685 --> 00:42:40.888
idempotency key 1,000 0 0 1,088 12. key reuse with a different amount: first, then 201, second, then 422 (1 mismatch).

658
00:42:43.738 --> 00:42:44.591
Reading it:

659
00:42:44.871 --> 00:42:45.703
Naive:

660
00:42:45.983 --> 00:42:54.218
1,657 charges for 1,000 orders, and 425 customers charged more than once.

661
00:42:54.498 --> 00:42:58.999
Every lost response turned into a retry, and every retry into a charge.

662
00:42:59.279 --> 00:43:00.367
Check-then-act:

663
00:43:00.647 --> 00:43:05.618
Handles sequential retries, because the key is there by the time the retry arrives.

664
00:43:06.968 --> 00:43:09.229
Fails exactly on the concurrent case:

665
00:43:09.509 --> 00:43:17.061
77 of the 100 double-clicks charged twice, because both requests passed the select before either inserted.

666
00:43:17.341 --> 00:43:22.291
This is the most common production implementation, and the one in §1.1.

667
00:43:22.571 --> 00:43:23.403
Atomic:

668
00:43:23.683 --> 00:43:26.477
1,000 charges and 0 duplicates.

669
00:43:26.757 --> 00:43:33.605
The concurrent duplicate blocks on the unique index until the first transaction commits, then reads the stored response.

670
00:43:34.955 --> 00:43:41.185
Client saw success is similar in all three runs, so you can't detect double charges from the client side.

671
00:43:41.465 --> 00:43:43.022
Only the ledger shows them.

672
00:43:43.302 --> 00:43:44.134
Gave up:

673
00:43:44.414 --> 00:43:52.542
9 to 12 clients exhausted their retries (0.3⁴ about 0.8 % lost 4 times in a row).

674
00:43:52.822 --> 00:43:56.449
In the atomic run, those orders were charged exactly once.

675
00:43:56.729 --> 00:44:02.403
The client must treat this as unknown and check status with the same key, never retry with a new key.

676
00:44:02.683 --> 00:44:07.697
Key reuse with a different amount is rejected (422), not replayed.

677
00:44:07.977 --> 00:44:15.187
Replaying would tell the client that 999 rupeeswas charged when 499 rupeeswas.

678
00:44:16.537 --> 00:44:21.679
L2, Idempotent consumers: naive versus Redis marker versus inbox.

679
00:44:21.959 --> 00:44:22.897
Requirement:

680
00:44:23.177 --> 00:44:30.239
The loyalty consumer must award exactly the right points, despite producer duplicates, crashes and redelivery.

681
00:44:30.519 --> 00:44:42.849
A log of 5,097 order.order.placed.v1 messages (2 % duplicated by a retrying producer) is consumed with offset commits every 100 messages.

682
00:44:43.129 --> 00:44:53.007
The consumer crashes at random points in its processing and restarts from the last committed offset, as a real Kafka consumer would after a crash or rebalance.

683
00:44:53.287 --> 00:45:00.817
Points are added to a real PostgreSQL table, and the Redis variant uses a real SET NX EX marker.

684
00:45:02.167 --> 00:45:05.154
Here is the Java code, ConsumerL2.java.

685
00:45:05.434 --> 00:45:09.445
It runs to 160 lines; the full listing is in the chapter.

686
00:45:09.725 --> 00:45:20.839
ConsumerL2.java, run with: java -Duser.timezone=UTC -cp or tmp or pgjdbc.jar ConsumerL2.java.

687
00:45:21.119 --> 00:45:30.911
An at-least-once consumer of order.order.placed.v1 awards loyalty points in real PostgreSQL (:5542).

688
00:45:31.191 --> 00:45:39.298
The log contains producer-retry duplicates (2 %), offsets are committed every 100 messages, and the consumer.

689
00:45:42.148 --> 00:45:43.577
On screen: log:

690
00:45:43.857 --> 00:45:51.580
5,097 messages (5,000 orders + 97 producer duplicates), offsets committed every.

691
00:45:51.860 --> 00:45:56.681
100, random crashes. strategy crashes points over- under- customers.

692
00:45:56.961 --> 00:46:09.676
(expected 269K) awarded awarded wrong. naive 15 308,349 38,642 0 189.

693
00:46:09.956 --> 00:46:25.145
Redis marker, then DB 10 269,548 0 159 3. inbox in same transaction 9 269,707 0 0 0.

694
00:46:27.995 --> 00:46:28.849
Reading it:

695
00:46:29.129 --> 00:46:29.961
Naive:

696
00:46:30.241 --> 00:46:43.531
189 of 200 customers got the wrong balance, with +38,642 points from redelivered batches (up to 99 messages per crash) and producer duplicates.

697
00:46:43.811 --> 00:46:45.561
Redis marker, then DB:

698
00:46:45.841 --> 00:46:50.107
Duplicates are gone, but 159 points are silently lost.

699
00:46:50.387 --> 00:46:58.835
A crash after SET NX and before the DB update marks the event as processed without applying it, and the redelivery then skips it.

700
00:47:00.185 --> 00:47:03.791
Two systems without a shared transaction can't be made atomic.

701
00:47:04.071 --> 00:47:08.060
This is the dual-write problem from Ch 13 in another form.

702
00:47:08.340 --> 00:47:10.943
Inbox in the same transaction: exact.

703
00:47:11.223 --> 00:47:16.620
The crash rolls back the inbox row and the points together, and the redelivery applies them once.

704
00:47:17.970 --> 00:47:21.874
L3, ID schemes: index cost and generator properties.

705
00:47:22.154 --> 00:47:23.093
Requirement:

706
00:47:23.373 --> 00:47:30.711
Choose primary keys for tables that will reach hundreds of millions of rows, and IDs for events generated on many pods.

707
00:47:30.991 --> 00:47:38.053
Part A inserts 4,000,000 orders with 8 writer threads (batches of 1,000) under four primary-key schemes.

708
00:47:38.333 --> 00:47:45.671
It measures insert time, primary-key index size, leaf density (pgstatindex) and the WAL generated.

709
00:47:45.951 --> 00:48:01.887
PostgreSQL runs with sharedbuffers = 32MB and maxwalsize = 256MB, so the index outgrows the buffer cache (as a production table's does at a much larger size) and checkpoints happen during the load.

710
00:48:02.167 --> 00:48:05.922
A single-writer run with 1M rows is the negative control.

