749
00:51:29.007 --> 00:51:29.860
Reading it:

750
00:51:30.140 --> 00:51:42.407
UUIDv4 is 2.3x slower to insert than UUIDv7 and writes 2.2x the WAL (1,514 MB versus 705 MB).

751
00:51:42.687 --> 00:51:45.097
Every insert lands on a random leaf page.

752
00:51:45.377 --> 00:51:54.892
Once the index doesn't fit in memory, pages are read back in, and after each checkpoint the first change to each page writes a full-page image to WAL.

753
00:51:55.172 --> 00:51:57.775
With random keys, that's nearly every page.

754
00:51:58.055 --> 00:52:04.881
More WAL also means more replication traffic and bigger backups (Ch 10, Ch 27).

755
00:52:06.231 --> 00:52:10.562
Ordered keys only touch the right edge of the index, which stays in memory.

756
00:52:10.842 --> 00:52:12.335
Leaf density surprise:

757
00:52:12.615 --> 00:52:21.191
With 8 concurrent writers, Snowflake and UUIDv7 indexes are less dense (61 %) than UUIDv4 (71 %).

758
00:52:21.471 --> 00:52:30.367
Application-generated ordered keys arrive slightly out of order from concurrent batches, so pages just left of the edge get 50 or 50 splits.

759
00:52:30.647 --> 00:52:34.615
The single-writer control shows 90 % for all ordered schemes.

760
00:52:35.965 --> 00:52:41.597
A DB-assigned identity, assigned at insert time, stays at 91 % either way.

761
00:52:41.877 --> 00:52:45.397
Bigger index, faster inserts: know which one you're optimising.

762
00:52:45.677 --> 00:52:46.616
Snowflake:

763
00:52:46.896 --> 00:52:53.424
Unique across 8 threads and 2 pods at ~0.8 to 1.2M ids per second.

764
00:52:53.704 --> 00:52:57.267
With a shared worker id, 20 % of ids collided.

765
00:52:57.547 --> 00:53:05.547
With an unguarded 5 ms clock step back, 10,000 duplicates (5 ms times 2,000 ids per millisecond).

766
00:53:05.827 --> 00:53:07.128
With the guard, 0.

767
00:53:08.478 --> 00:53:09.267
Ordering:

768
00:53:09.547 --> 00:53:12.790
UUIDv4 is sorted 50 % of the time (random).

769
00:53:13.070 --> 00:53:16.441
A monotonic v7 generator is 100 %.

770
00:53:16.721 --> 00:53:18.257
Short codes collide early:

771
00:53:18.537 --> 00:53:24.958
6 base32 characters (30 bits) collide after ~41 to 55K codes.

772
00:53:25.238 --> 00:53:32.129
The theory says √(π or 2, 2³⁰) about 41K, and the 20-trial average is noisy.

773
00:53:32.409 --> 00:53:36.462
Random public codes always need a unique constraint and a retry.

774
00:53:37.812 --> 00:53:40.372
L4, Distributed locks under pauses:

775
00:53:40.652 --> 00:53:43.447
TTL, fencing, advisory locks, CAS.

776
00:53:43.727 --> 00:53:44.665
Requirement:

777
00:53:44.945 --> 00:53:51.921
Store 17's settlement must be updated by one worker at a time, even when a worker is paused longer than its lock.

778
00:53:52.201 --> 00:54:00.884
8 workers each perform 150 read-modify-write increments of a PostgreSQL row (1,200 expected).

779
00:54:01.164 --> 00:54:09.740
Redis locks have a 100 ms TTL, and 3 % of critical sections pause 150 ms between the read and the write.

780
00:54:10.020 --> 00:54:18.041
The last lines are a scripted interleaving that random pauses rarely produce, plus a lock without a TTL whose holder crashes.

781
00:54:19.391 --> 00:54:22.335
