784
00:54:37.917 --> 00:54:50.482
8 workers each add 1 to a store's settlement counter 150 times with a read-modify-write on real PostgreSQL (:5542). guarded by different locks.

785
00:54:50.762 --> 00:55:00.234
Redis (:6390) locks have a 100 ms TTL, and 3 % of critical sections suffer a 150 ms.

786
00:55:03.084 --> 00:55:04.001
On screen:

787
00:55:04.281 --> 00:55:28.623
8 workers times 150 increments (expected 1,200); lock TTL 100 ms; 3 % of critical sections. pause 150 ms. scheme final lost 2 holders foreign rejected time. value at once unlocks and retried. no lock 152 1,048 0 0 0 1,540 ms.

788
00:55:28.903 --> 00:55:41.020
Redis TTL lock, DEL release 510 690 1172 307 0 2,749 ms.

789
00:55:41.300 --> 00:56:10.969
Redis TTL lock, owner-checked release 776 424 851 0 0 4,429 ms. + fencing token checked on write 1,200 0 833 0 36 5,200 ms. + fencing token claimed at read 1,200 0 857 0 42 6,386 ms.

790
00:56:11.249 --> 00:56:32.518
PostgreSQL advisory xact lock 1,200 0 0 0 0 7,223 ms. no lock, optimistic CAS + retry 1,200 0 0 0 769 1,804 ms. scripted pause, fencing token checked on write:

791
00:56:32.798 --> 00:56:38.067
A's stale write accepted, B's write. accepted, then value 1 after 2 increments.

792
00:56:38.347 --> 00:56:39.862
The rest is in the chapter.

793
00:56:42.712 --> 00:56:43.565
Reading it:

794
00:56:43.845 --> 00:56:44.699
No lock:

795
00:56:44.979 --> 00:56:49.907
1,048 of 1,200 increments lost, as a baseline.

796
00:56:50.187 --> 00:56:53.088
Redis TTL lock with plain DEL release:

797
00:56:53.368 --> 00:56:56.931
The paused holder's lock expires and another worker takes it.

798
00:56:57.211 --> 00:57:06.171
When the first wakes, it writes a stale value and deletes the new holder's lock (307 foreign unlocks), which cascades into more overlap.

799
00:57:06.451 --> 00:57:08.499
690 increments lost.

800
00:57:09.849 --> 00:57:14.947
Owner-checked release (Lua compare-and-delete) stops the foreign unlocks but not the overlap.

801
00:57:15.227 --> 00:57:20.262
Pauses still create two holders at once, and 424 increments were lost.

802
00:57:20.542 --> 00:57:22.995
A TTL lock is an efficiency lock.

803
00:57:23.275 --> 00:57:24.449
Fencing tokens:

804
00:57:24.729 --> 00:57:27.395
Both variants lost 0 in the random run.

805
00:57:27.675 --> 00:57:33.755
The resource rejected 36 to 42 stale operations, which then retried under a new lease.

806
00:57:35.105 --> 00:57:37.388
The scripted interleaving shows the difference.

807
00:57:37.668 --> 00:57:46.820
With the token checked only on the write, A's stale write is accepted because no newer token had written yet, so the value is 1 after 2 increments.

808
00:57:47.100 --> 00:57:51.943
With the token claimed at read, A is rejected and retries, and the value is 2.

809
00:57:52.223 --> 00:57:55.081
PostgreSQL advisory transaction lock:

810
00:57:55.361 --> 00:58:02.380
0 lost and no overlaps, because the lock lives in the same database as the data and has no TTL.

811
00:58:02.660 --> 00:58:04.516
A paused holder simply keeps it.

812
00:58:04.796 --> 00:58:11.303
The cost is liveness: it was the slowest option (7.2 s), since each pause stalls everyone.

813
00:58:11.583 --> 00:58:13.503
Set session and statement timeouts.

814
00:58:14.853 --> 00:58:20.762
No lock, optimistic CAS: update... where value =:read, retried on conflict.

815
00:58:21.042 --> 00:58:24.925
0 lost, and the fastest correct option (1.8 s).

816
00:58:25.205 --> 00:58:29.493
769 retries is the cost under this contention.

817
00:58:29.773 --> 00:58:33.549
When the resource can do a conditional write, the lock is unnecessary.

818
00:58:33.829 --> 00:58:39.418
A lock without a TTL whose holder crashes is held forever (PTTL -1).

819
00:58:39.698 --> 00:58:44.370
TTLs are required for liveness, and fencing is required for safety.

