826
01:00:15.815 --> 01:00:17.095
Production Concerns.

827
01:00:18.345 --> 01:00:19.497
Failure modes.

828
01:00:19.777 --> 01:00:21.121
Here is a quick table:

829
01:00:21.401 --> 01:00:24.388
Failure, and Symptom, and Cause, and Mitigation.

830
01:00:24.668 --> 01:00:26.652
Double charge or double order.

831
01:00:26.932 --> 01:00:27.743
Symptom:

832
01:00:28.023 --> 01:00:30.305
Ledger has two charges for one order.

833
01:00:30.585 --> 01:00:31.353
Cause:

834
01:00:31.633 --> 01:00:36.433
Retry without a key, check-then-act, key generated per HTTP attempt.

835
01:00:36.713 --> 01:00:37.716
Mitigation:

836
01:00:37.996 --> 01:00:42.604
Atomic claim (L1), client persists the key per logical operation.

837
01:00:42.884 --> 01:00:43.951
Lost effect.

838
01:00:44.231 --> 01:00:45.041
Symptom:

839
01:00:45.321 --> 01:00:47.945
Points or stock missing, no error anywhere.

840
01:00:48.225 --> 01:00:48.993
Cause:

841
01:00:49.273 --> 01:00:52.409
Dedup marker in a different store than the effect (L2).

842
01:00:52.689 --> 01:00:53.692
Mitigation:

843
01:00:53.972 --> 01:00:55.743
Inbox in the same transaction.

844
01:00:56.023 --> 01:00:57.516
Stuck INPROGRESS records.

845
01:00:57.796 --> 01:00:58.607
Symptom:

846
01:00:58.887 --> 01:01:01.681
409s for a key that never completes.

847
01:01:01.961 --> 01:01:02.729
Cause:

848
01:01:03.009 --> 01:01:06.380
Crash after an external call, before recording the result.

849
01:01:06.660 --> 01:01:07.663
Mitigation:

850
01:01:07.943 --> 01:01:12.188
Recovery points + reconciler; timeout, then resume or fail.

851
01:01:12.468 --> 01:01:14.260
False 422s.

852
01:01:14.540 --> 01:01:15.351
Symptom:

853
01:01:15.631 --> 01:01:17.359
Legit retries rejected.

854
01:01:17.639 --> 01:01:18.407
Cause:

855
01:01:18.687 --> 01:01:22.484
Fingerprint includes volatile fields (timestamps, header order).

856
01:01:22.764 --> 01:01:23.767
Mitigation:

857
01:01:24.047 --> 01:01:27.225
Canonical fingerprint of business fields; contract tests.

858
01:01:28.575 --> 01:01:30.218
Idempotency table bloat.

859
01:01:30.498 --> 01:01:31.309
Symptom:

860
01:01:31.589 --> 01:01:33.338
Slow claims, huge table.

861
01:01:33.618 --> 01:01:34.386
Cause:

862
01:01:34.666 --> 01:01:35.797
No retention.

863
01:01:36.077 --> 01:01:37.079
Mitigation:

864
01:01:37.359 --> 01:01:39.557
Daily partitions, drop by age.

865
01:01:39.837 --> 01:01:40.989
Duplicate IDs.

866
01:01:41.269 --> 01:01:42.079
Symptom:

867
01:01:42.359 --> 01:01:47.330
Unique violations, or worse, silent overwrites in stores without constraints.

868
01:01:47.610 --> 01:01:48.378
Cause:

869
01:01:48.658 --> 01:01:52.029
Shared Snowflake worker id, clock step back (L3).

870
01:01:52.309 --> 01:01:53.311
Mitigation:

871
01:01:53.591 --> 01:01:56.706
Leased worker ids, clock guards, unique constraints.

872
01:01:56.986 --> 01:01:59.290
Slow inserts, WAL growth.

873
01:01:59.570 --> 01:02:00.381
Symptom:

874
01:02:00.661 --> 01:02:03.157
Write latency rises as tables grow.

875
01:02:03.437 --> 01:02:04.205
Cause:

876
01:02:04.485 --> 01:02:06.618
Random UUIDv4 PKs (L3).

877
01:02:06.898 --> 01:02:07.901
Mitigation:

878
01:02:08.181 --> 01:02:11.231
UUIDv7 or identity; migrate hot tables.

879
01:02:11.511 --> 01:02:12.685
Two lock holders.

880
01:02:12.965 --> 01:02:13.775
Symptom:

881
01:02:14.055 --> 01:02:16.338
Double settlement, corrupted aggregate.

882
01:02:16.618 --> 01:02:17.386
Cause:

883
01:02:17.666 --> 01:02:19.991
TTL lock + pause (L4).

884
01:02:20.271 --> 01:02:21.274
Mitigation:

885
01:02:21.554 --> 01:02:24.562
Fencing claimed at read, DB-held locks, CAS.

886
01:02:25.912 --> 01:02:27.235
Lock never released.

887
01:02:27.515 --> 01:02:28.325
Symptom:

888
01:02:28.605 --> 01:02:29.949
Job stops running.

889
01:02:30.229 --> 01:02:30.997
Cause:

890
01:02:31.277 --> 01:02:33.837
No TTL + holder crash (L4).

891
01:02:34.117 --> 01:02:35.120
Mitigation:

892
01:02:35.400 --> 01:02:38.835
TTL or session-bound locks; alert on lock age.

893
01:02:39.115 --> 01:02:40.224
Foreign unlock.

894
01:02:40.504 --> 01:02:41.315
Symptom:

895
01:02:41.595 --> 01:02:42.896
Cascading overlaps.

896
01:02:43.176 --> 01:02:43.944
Cause:

897
01:02:44.224 --> 01:02:46.592
DEL without an owner check (L4).

898
01:02:46.872 --> 01:02:47.875
Mitigation:

899
01:02:48.155 --> 01:02:49.627
Compare-and-delete in Lua.

900
01:02:50.977 --> 01:02:52.065
Observability.

901
01:02:52.345 --> 01:03:04.249
Idempotency: replay rate per endpoint (a sudden rise means client or network trouble), 422 and 409 rates, INPROGRESS age histogram, records per day.

902
01:03:04.529 --> 01:03:08.006
Consumers: duplicates skipped per consumer (inbox hits).

903
01:03:08.286 --> 01:03:11.422
A rise signals rebalances or producer retries.

904
01:03:11.702 --> 01:03:16.417
Payments with unknown outcome: count and age, and reconciler resolutions.

905
01:03:17.767 --> 01:03:30.631
Locks: acquisition wait, hold time versus TTL (alert when hold time approaches the TTL), fencing rejections (each is a would-be incident), lock age for TTL-less locks.

906
01:03:30.911 --> 01:03:31.657
IDs:

907
01:03:31.937 --> 01:03:37.932
Snowflake clock-rollback waits, sequence exhaustion waits, worker-id lease renewals and losses.

908
01:03:38.212 --> 01:03:39.172
Database:

909
01:03:39.452 --> 01:03:44.295
WAL rate and index bloat for tables keyed by random UUIDs.

910
01:03:45.645 --> 01:03:46.583
Security.

911
01:03:46.863 --> 01:03:48.357
Scope keys per principal:

912
01:03:48.637 --> 01:03:49.789
(customerid, key).

913
01:03:50.069 --> 01:03:55.893
Otherwise one customer's key could replay another customer's stored response (an information leak).

914
01:03:56.173 --> 01:03:59.778
Stored responses may contain PII or payment data.

915
01:04:00.058 --> 01:04:04.346
Encrypt or minimise them, apply retention, and exclude them from logs.

916
01:04:04.626 --> 01:04:10.962
Public identifiers: non-enumerable references (random or v7 plus authorization checks).

917
01:04:11.242 --> 01:04:16.383
Sequential IDs in URLs invite IDOR scraping and reveal business volume.

918
01:04:16.663 --> 01:04:19.778
UUIDv7 and Snowflake leak the creation time.

919
01:04:20.058 --> 01:04:26.415
Usually fine for orders, but not for everything (for example, account IDs that reveal signup dates).

920
01:04:27.765 --> 01:04:31.435
Lock keys in shared Redis need a namespace and ACLs.

921
01:04:31.715 --> 01:04:35.597
Anyone who can DEL your lock can break mutual exclusion.

922
01:04:36.947 --> 01:04:38.611
Performance and tuning knobs.

923
01:04:38.891 --> 01:04:40.235
Here is a quick table:

924
01:04:40.515 --> 01:04:41.731
Knob, and Guidance.

925
01:04:42.011 --> 01:04:43.355
Idempotency claim:

926
01:04:43.635 --> 01:04:47.433
One insert... on conflict on a PK index: sub-ms.

927
01:04:47.713 --> 01:04:50.507
Keep the record in the same DB as the effect.

928
01:04:50.787 --> 01:04:52.110
Concurrent duplicates:

929
01:04:52.390 --> 01:04:57.638
Same-DB claim: the second waits on the index lock (bounded by the first transaction).

930
01:04:57.918 --> 01:04:59.411
Keep transactions short.

931
01:04:59.691 --> 01:05:00.865
Inbox inserts:

932
01:05:01.145 --> 01:05:10.382
Composite PK (consumer, eventid); batch consumers can insert many at once (insert... select unnest(?) on conflict do nothing returning).

933
01:05:10.662 --> 01:05:11.857
Primary key type:

934
01:05:12.137 --> 01:05:15.145
UUIDv7 (16 B) or bigint (8 B).

935
01:05:15.425 --> 01:05:17.942
Every FK and secondary index repeats it.

936
01:05:18.222 --> 01:05:19.694
Snowflake bit split:

937
01:05:19.974 --> 01:05:27.761
41 or 10 or 12 default; fewer worker bits if you have fewer pods, more sequence bits for bursty generators.

