01:10:06.231 --> 01:10:07.255
Question 1.

1000
01:10:07.535 --> 01:10:11.525
What does idempotent mean, and why does it matter in distributed systems?

1001
01:10:11.805 --> 01:10:13.170
Thirty-second answer:

1002
01:10:13.450 --> 01:10:19.253
An operation is idempotent if doing it several times has the same effect as doing it once.

1003
01:10:19.533 --> 01:10:27.511
It matters because networks lose responses: a client that times out can't tell whether the server processed the request, so it retries.

1004
01:10:27.791 --> 01:10:34.085
If the operation isn't idempotent, the retry duplicates the effect (a second charge, a second order).

1005
01:10:35.435 --> 01:10:36.437
Question 2.

1006
01:10:36.717 --> 01:10:38.360
What is an idempotency key?

1007
01:10:38.640 --> 01:10:40.005
Thirty-second answer:

1008
01:10:40.285 --> 01:10:48.648
A unique value the client generates for one logical operation and sends with every attempt, usually in an Idempotency-Key header.

1009
01:10:48.928 --> 01:10:55.883
The server remembers the keys it has processed and, for a repeat, returns the stored result instead of doing the work again.

1010
01:10:57.233 --> 01:10:58.278
