1028
01:12:02.254 --> 01:12:09.529
Why are random UUIDs bad primary keys in PostgreSQL or MySQL, and what do you use instead?

1029
01:12:09.809 --> 01:12:11.174
Thirty-second answer:

1030
01:12:11.454 --> 01:12:15.251
B-tree indexes stay efficient when new keys go to the right edge.

1031
01:12:15.531 --> 01:12:18.667
Random UUIDs insert everywhere in the index.

1032
01:12:18.947 --> 01:12:27.801
Once it's bigger than memory, inserts read random pages, cause page splits, and write full-page images to WAL after every checkpoint.

1033
01:12:28.081 --> 01:12:28.934
