1056
01:13:46.705 --> 01:13:50.651
What is a fencing token, and why isn't a lock with a TTL enough?

1057
01:13:50.931 --> 01:13:52.297
Thirty-second answer:

1058
01:13:52.577 --> 01:13:58.401
A number that increases with every lock grant, sent with every operation on the protected resource.

1059
01:13:58.681 --> 01:14:03.737
The resource rejects operations carrying a token older than the newest it has seen.

1060
01:14:04.017 --> 01:14:10.033
It's needed because a holder can pause (GC, VM steal) beyond its TTL without knowing.

1061
01:14:10.313 --> 01:14:14.537
Another process gets the lock, and the paused one wakes up and writes anyway.

