01:14:45.031 --> 01:14:49.106
Two requests with the same idempotency key arrive at the same time.

1071
01:14:49.386 --> 01:14:50.858
What happens in your design?

1072
01:14:51.138 --> 01:14:52.503
Thirty-second answer:

1073
01:14:52.783 --> 01:14:56.453
Both try insert... on conflict do nothing on the unique key.

1074
01:14:56.733 --> 01:15:04.690
PostgreSQL lets one insert, and the other blocks on the index entry until the first transaction commits or rolls back.

1075
01:15:04.970 --> 01:15:09.173
On commit, the second sees the conflict and replays the stored response.

1076
01:15:09.453 --> 01:15:13.826
On rollback, the second's insert succeeds and it processes the request.

