1097
01:16:19.541 --> 01:16:22.123
Explain the Redlock debate and your position.

1098
01:16:22.403 --> 01:16:23.768
Thirty-second answer:

1099
01:16:24.048 --> 01:16:29.509
Redlock acquires a lock on a majority of N independent Redis nodes with a TTL.

1100
01:16:29.789 --> 01:16:41.203
Kleppmann argued it's unsafe for correctness: there's no fencing token, and safety depends on bounded process pauses, network delays and clock drift, which real systems violate.

1101
01:16:41.483 --> 01:16:46.112
Antirez argued the assumptions are reasonable and timing checks mitigate them.

1102
01:16:46.392 --> 01:16:50.893
My position: use Redlock or any TTL lock only for efficiency.

1103
01:16:51.173 --> 01:16:59.408
For correctness, use a consensus system with fencing tokens checked by the resource, or let the database enforce the invariant.

